.TH ASMOND 1 "October 29, 2026" "Asmond 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
asmond \- macOS power, thermal and activity monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B asmond
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B asmond
.RI [ options ]
.B probe
.RI [ --raw ]
.br
.B asmond
.RI [ options ]
.B doctor
.RI [ --json ]
.RI [ --live ]
.br
.B asmond
.RI [ options ]
.B report
.RI [ --json ]
.RI [ --live ]
.br
.B asmond record
.RI [ --interval " seconds" ]
.RI [ --groups " names" ]
.RI [ --output-dir " directory" ]
.RI [ --duration " seconds" ]
.RI [ --max-size-mib " MiB" ]
.br
.B asmond export
.I session-directory
.BI --output-dir " new-directory"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Asmond
is a compact terminal monitor for macOS, focused on Apple Silicon with early
experimental Intel Mac support.
It combines Apple's
.BR powermetrics (1)
plist stream with system counters for memory, swap, battery, USB-C/MagSafe
charging, disk and network I/O, and processes.
.PP
The dashboard shows SoC or Intel package, CPU, GPU and ANE/NPU power where
macOS exposes those values, thermal pressure and throttling state, CPU/GPU
load, clocks, verified CPU/GPU temperatures, the hottest verified thermal
sensor with its source, RAM and
swap pressure, battery details, charging port information and optional process
and I/O panels.
.PP
Panel borders use rounded Unicode corners where the terminal accepts them,
unless Rounded borders is disabled in the settings menu.
The complete border uses one matching Unicode glyph set so its lines meet the
corners cleanly. If the terminal cannot render that set, Asmond draws one
complete square curses border instead.
.PP
Graph cells use an eleven-step green-to-yellow-to-red palette when the terminal
supports at least 256 colors. Less capable terminals automatically use the
existing green, yellow and red thresholds. The intentionally monochrome
.B mono
theme never enables the gradient. Power and I/O graph colors are relative to
the current graph scale and do not by themselves indicate a critical hardware
condition.
.PP
The terminal UI stays unprivileged. Asmond starts only
.BR powermetrics (1)
through
.BR sudo (8)
when live privileged telemetry is required.
.PP
Asmond invokes privileged and system telemetry tools through their fixed macOS
paths. The sudo credential timestamp may remain valid after Asmond exits;
Asmond does not revoke a credential that may have existed before it started.
The explicit root UI override does not write user settings.
.SH RECORDING
The record command runs headlessly, without changing the TUI.
Start it manually as the normal user. Only the selected power group requests
sudo for powermetrics; the recorder itself is not elevated.
The interval after record is 1 to 3600 seconds, default 10, independent of
saved dashboard settings. Groups are comma-separated cpu, power, temperatures,
memory, processes, io and battery; all are selected by default.
.PP
Each invocation creates a new private session directory below
~/Library/Logs/Asmond, or the chosen output parent. Files rotate by UTC day.
The total JSONL limit defaults to 256 MiB. Reaching it stops recording;
existing data is never overwritten or automatically deleted. Ctrl+C and
SIGTERM request shutdown; duration optionally limits the run. There is no
automatic restart/resume, database, network listener or process action.
.PP
Two independent top-ten lists rank interval CPU time and resident memory.
PID/start time, process basename, CPU percentage, RAM percentage and RSS are
recorded, but not arguments, full paths or user names. Multithreaded processes
can exceed 100 percent CPU. The first interval and reused/reset identities
have no fabricated CPU percentage.
.PP
Memory includes compressor occupancy and content, swap occupancy and real
Swapins/Swapouts/Compressions/Decompressions counter deltas using the reported
page size. Missing reads, resets and long gaps reset rate baselines instead
of inventing zero activity. Pressure percentage retains its existing labelled
memory_pressure inverse-free definition, not a native pressure level.
State-transition events use existing alert thresholds and source availability.
.PP
After recording stops, export creates metrics.csv, processes.csv and events.csv
in a new private directory. Missing cells remain empty, formula-like text is
escaped, and malformed/incomplete input is rejected. An unsuccessful export
may leave a private partial directory. Logs remain sensitive, not anonymized
support reports. See docs/recording.md in the source distribution.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --version
Print the Asmond version and exit.
.TP
.B --update
Update an installation managed by Asmond's direct installer, with confirmation.
The installer validates and tests a complete release before atomically switching
the active version. Settings are preserved. Homebrew installations must use
brew upgrade asmond; source checkouts must use their existing update workflow.
Direct installation and updates require the matching official release archive
and checksum assets, available starting with release 0.9.0.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show command-line help and exit.
.TP
.BI -i " seconds" ", --interval " seconds
Set the sample interval in seconds. Values are clamped to Asmond's supported
range.
.TP
.BI --history " samples"
Set how many samples are kept for graphs.
.TP
.BI -t " theme" ", --theme " theme
Choose a color theme. Available themes are:
.BR classic ,
.BR matrix ,
.BR solar ,
.BR mono ,
.BR nord ,
.BR dracula ,
.BR ocean
and
.BR ember .
.TP
.BI --layout " layout"
Choose the dashboard layout.
.I layout
may be
.BR full ,
.BR compact ,
.B focus
or
.BR custom .
The
.B focus
layout is the combined power and thermal view.
The
.B custom
layout starts from the full grid and can be edited with Tailor mode
.RB ( T ).
.TP
.B --show-io
Show the compact disk/network I/O panel on startup.
.TP
.B --mock
Run with generated demo data instead of live
.BR powermetrics (1)
data. This is useful for checking the interface without elevated telemetry.
.TP
.B --remove-settings
Remove the saved user settings file and exit.
.TP
.B --config-path
Print the settings file path and exit.
.TP
.B --allow-root-ui
Allow the full terminal UI to run as root. This is disabled by default; normal
interactive use should run
.B asmond
as the user and let Asmond elevate only
.BR powermetrics (1).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B probe
Print one parsed
.BR powermetrics (1)
sample and exit.
.TP
.B probe --raw
Print one parsed sample and then list flattened raw plist keys and values. This
helps when checking which counters a macOS version exposes. The output is not
anonymized; use
.B report
for public support output.
.TP
.B doctor
Check local dependencies and data sources, including macOS architecture,
Python version, required system commands, settings path writability and cached
sudo state. The command does not prompt for sudo.
.TP
.B doctor --json
Print diagnostics as JSON.
.TP
.B doctor --live
Also try one live
.BR powermetrics (1)
sample when sudo is already cached.
.TP
.B report
Print an anonymized support report in Markdown. The report includes system
version, diagnostics and non-sensitive memory, battery and USB-C/MagSafe
summary data.
.TP
.B report --json
Print the support report as JSON.
.TP
.B report --live
Include one live
.BR powermetrics (1)
sample. This may prompt for sudo.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B ? / h
Toggle the help overlay.
.TP
.B m
Toggle the settings menu.
Up/Down wraps from the first entry to the last and back in menus,
Tailor, Data sources and the process list. Moving the process selection
cancels a pending TERM confirmation.
.TP
.B t
Cycle the color theme.
.TP
.B T
Open the Tailor menu directly from any layout, switching to custom.
Up/Down selects a row; Left/Right changes a value; Enter applies an action.
Esc or T closes the menu. Numbers 1-7 directly select an existing field of the
active grid and focus its panel row; Field also supports arrow-only selection.
Profile selects a named configuration; Duplicate profile makes an independent
copy and Rename profile edits its name. Its index/count clarifies when only
the initial Default profile exists. Up to 16 profiles can be stored.
Delete profile requires confirmation and selects another existing profile;
the last remaining profile is protected.
Each profile remembers its grid and separate assignments for Classic
(seven fields), Balanced (2x2) and Columns (3x2, or 2x3 below 144 columns).
Existing custom settings become the first profile without losing assignments.
Field selects an area; Custom panel and Custom detail change its content.
Swap with field exchanges assignments; Copy to field overwrites the chosen
target only after confirmation. Reset grid restores only the active profile's
current grid, also after confirmation. Esc cancels a confirmation.
The editor shows the field size and warns about hidden or small fields.
Battery, Power Input and Cable can be assigned independently.
These fixed blocks show unavailable telemetry rather than changing source;
the original Charge block still follows
.B b
and its capability-aware fallback. Power graph shows or hides the wide graph
per profile, returning its space to the other fields when hidden.
Built-in Full, Compact and Focus layouts are unchanged.
.PP
The settings menu also contains Data sources: a read-only inspector for received
values and source metadata. Enter opens it; Up/Down scrolls; Esc returns.
It never starts extra probes or requests additional privileges.
Mock data, derived values and unavailable readings remain explicit.
Reader-group metadata is not presented as per-sensor provenance, and n/a does
not prove an unsupported capability. Per-field freshness/read status is not
available for every model field; this is not a raw-value validation tool.
.TP
.B + / -
Change and apply the sample interval.
.TP
.B r
Reset power graph history and peak values.
.TP
.B v
Cycle the layout preset:
.BR full ,
.BR compact ,
.B focus
or
.BR custom .
.TP
.B d
Show or hide the disk/network I/O panel.
.TP
.B i / o
Cycle the upper and lower disk/network graph sources.
.TP
.B L
Toggle CPU/GPU average rows versus average graph view.
.TP
.B b
Cycle the charge panel between Battery, Power Input, Cable and Port diagnostics.
Port diagnostics is skipped when Port diag in b is off in the settings menu.
.TP
.B S C G A
Select SoC/package, CPU, GPU or ANE/NPU for the upper power graph.
.TP
.B s c g a
Select SoC/package, CPU, GPU or ANE/NPU for the lower power graph.
.TP
.B u / n
Cycle the upper and lower power graph sources.
.TP
.B p
Cycle the process panel:
.BR hidden ,
.B left
or
.BR right .
.TP
.B Up / Down
Select a process when the process panel is visible.
.TP
.B Left / Right
Change process sorting in column order:
PID, CPU, GPU (when shown), RAM, RSS and command.
.TP
.B k
Mark the selected process; press
.B k
again during the confirmation window to send
.BR TERM .
.SH SETTINGS MENU
In the settings menu, use
.B Up
and
.B Down
or
.B Tab
to move, use
.B Left
and
.B Right
or
.B Enter
to change a value, press
.B s
to save and press
.B Esc
to close.
.PP
Rounded borders switches dashboard and overlay boxes between rounded Unicode
and square curses borders. Port diag in b includes or skips port diagnostics
in the charge-panel cycle and its next-view hint. Both preferences are saved
automatically and default to on for compatibility with existing settings.
Diagnostics remains accessible directly from the menu and as a fixed Tailor
block. Disabling the cycle option while diagnostics is active restores the
previous charge view. With that option off, a saved diagnostics selection
starts on Battery after restart. Neither preference disables telemetry or
reported-liquid warnings.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Asmond/settings.json
Saved UI settings, including theme, interval, layout, custom slots, graph
sources, process panel mode and alert thresholds.
.PP
If Asmond is explicitly launched through
.BR sudo (8)
for a non-dashboard command, the settings path is resolved through
.B SUDO_USER
so the file still belongs to the real user.
.SH NOTES
Asmond prefers exposed macOS counters over estimates. Some counters are not
publicly available on every system or macOS version. Missing values are shown as
unavailable instead of being invented.
.PP
Powermetrics values marked invalid, negative sentinel values and non-finite
numbers are discarded. If the main stream stops, the dashboard marks the data
stale and retries with bounded backoff.
.PP
Thermal pressure is shown exactly as macOS reports it. A throttled state is set
only from an explicitly active throttle, PROCHOT or Plimit signal, or from a
macOS pressure state of Serious, Heavy or Critical. Intel perf_limit_reasons
are displayed separately as Perf reason because powermetrics does not identify
those strings as current versus latched state. Normal turbo-control reasons can
also appear while thermal pressure is Nominal, so Asmond preserves them without
inferring thermal throttling.
.PP
On a base Apple M4 with four performance and six efficiency cores, CPU and GPU
temperature averages and maxima are read from verified, read-only AppleSMC
sensor groups. The averages are shown beside CPU/GPU power; no temperature is
invented for the SoC or ANE rows. The Thermals panel shows the hottest verified
sensor with its source and can also show the Power Manager Die average plus
available power-supply, Wi-Fi/AirPort and trackpad sensors. A hottest CPU
sensor includes its verified zero-based efficiency/performance core, such as
CPU@E5.
Lower PMU tdie readings remain power-manager-die sensors and are not presented
as CPU-core or SoC temperatures. The tested M4 exposes no named SoC or ANE
temperature through IOHID, and Asmond does not relabel CPU-die or shared
low-confidence sensors to fill those rows. The M4 mapping is not extrapolated
to other SoCs or core topologies. AppleSMC monitoring requires neither sudo
nor a third-party package. At dashboard intervals below 0.75 seconds, the
latest real SMC/IOHID read is reused until the next 0.75-second sensor refresh
so a slow hardware read does not block the faster powermetrics pipe. Values
are not interpolated or estimated.
.PP
On Apple Silicon, available fan speeds use AppleSMC FNum and F#Ac actual-RPM
keys. Each fan retains its numeric hardware index; no left/right assignment,
aggregate, target or minimum-speed substitute is invented. Genuine zero RPM
remains a reading. Native Intel Macs use the same read-only indexed fan path
when available, retaining powermetrics as the legacy scalar fallback.
.PP
On the M2 Max 8P+4E, Data sources, probe and live report additionally expose
unassigned raw SMC temperature keys for comparison. Zero and sentinel-like
values may be present. These keys are excluded from CPU/GPU summaries, the
hotspot and alerts; their hardware assignment has not been validated.
.PP
On the tested Mac14,5 M2 Max with 8P+4E, an independently compared auxiliary
map additionally enables AirPort, trackpad and trackpad-actuator temperatures.
It does not enable CPU/GPU/core or power-supply-temperature assignments and
is not applied to other M2 chassis. Named auxiliary keys are omitted from the
unassigned raw list. Neither the M4 CPU average nor any M2 CPU average is
derived from TCMb; the M4 averages its individually verified sensor group.
.PP
Battery health uses the nominal/full and design capacities exposed by
.BR AppleSmartBattery ,
including the nested BatteryData fields used by newer macOS versions. Battery
temperature prefers the existing AppleSmartBattery temperature fields and
falls back to Apple's IOHID gas-gauge battery sensors when those fields are
absent.
.PP
In the full dashboard, the compact four-row Battery view gives its unused two
lines to the RAM panel. The Power Input and Cable views keep the larger panel
for their additional fields. Cycling between the views recalculates this
allocation immediately.
.PP
Apple Silicon is the stable primary platform. Intel Mac support is experimental
and intentionally conservative until it has been exercised across more
machines and macOS versions. On tested
Mac mini and MacBook Air hardware Asmond can read a smaller set of Intel
.BR powermetrics (1)
counters such as package/CPU/iGPU power, CPU/GPU load and clocks. CPU-die
temperature, fan speed, battery and legacy MagSafe telemetry are also shown
when the hardware exposes them. SMC readings may be temporarily unavailable
on individual samples.
.PP
Per-process GPU percentage is a legacy, best-effort path. It uses
.B powermetrics --show-process-gpu
when the process panel is visible, but Apple documents this as available only on
certain hardware. macOS exposes this as GPU time rather than per-app GPU power.
Current tested Apple Silicon/macOS builds may omit the GPU column from plist
output or report only zeroes in text output, so Asmond falls back to parsing the
.B GPU ms/s
text column when useful and hides the process GPU column when the counter is
unavailable.
.PP
USB-C, MagSafe and legacy adapter charge information is decoded from the
.B AppleSmartBattery
and
.B AppleHPMInterface
IOKit trees, with AppleTCController as a fallback when the primary port class
is empty. Physical port identities are retained; the two sources are not merged
by enumeration order. Live voltage, current and wattage prefer measured
telemetry when
available and fall back to negotiated adapter or USB-PD values. Fixed,
battery, variable and PPS request objects are decoded only with their matching
USB-PD field layout. AppleHPMInterface ConnectionActive is correlated with the
global input state and per-port contract evidence; by itself it proves only a
cable or data connection and never turns that port into a power sink.
.PP
Port diagnostics opens directly through the existing charge-view cycle or
the settings menu, without an Enter step or popup. It uses the normal charge
box, capped at eight terminal rows, and shows the selected port only once
in its header. A few values are visible at a time; the rest are scrollable.
Left/Right selects ports or explicitly unmapped controllers; Up/Down scrolls
and wraps; b continues to Battery; Esc restores the previous charge view.
Menus and help take keyboard priority. Other visible panels keep polling;
process actions and selection markers are disabled and pending TERM cancelled.
Tailor temporarily shows the saved grid for editing, without overwriting it.
Independent Tailor diagnostics blocks remain compact summaries.
When there is no charge slot, a suitable existing field is temporarily used;
saved assignments are not changed.
Liquid detection displays reported HPM controller state, not a physical
inspection. A positive report also appears in the dashboard status line.
Missing diagnostic fields, blank status text and all-zero event-buffer
sections are hidden until data arrives. Zero counters and explicit false
states remain visible. Unknown port identity stays explicit in the header;
missing data is not a clean bill of health. Other panels and the data-source
inspector retain their existing missing-value behavior.
Counters are reported events, not lifetime wear or proof of cable damage.
Controller entries without explicit, unambiguous identities remain unmapped;
array order is never used to assign diagnostics to physical ports.
Session logs record observation times, may miss transitions, and are bounded
to 64 entries per mapped source. Counter decreases start a new baseline.
Firmware event buffers are shown as raw hex without speculative decoding.
.PP
The Graph view setting selects power or charging in the existing wide graph.
Charging plots measured SystemPowerIn and signed battery power only; positive
battery power means charging and negative power means discharge.
Input is green, battery power uses the accent color, and overlapping cells
are neutral. Missing values remain gaps, genuine zero remains zero, and
negotiated contracts and charger ratings are never substituted.
Stale header values are hidden and their age is shown. History is session-only
and is cleared by the existing power-history reset. A fluctuating curve is
not by itself evidence of a faulty cable.
.PP
When a Cable view is visible with multiple connected, uniquely identified
ports, Left/Right cycles them and wraps. The Port row shows the selection and
count, including data-only connections without E-marker data. Selection is
session-only and follows the physical port across registry reordering;
unplugging the selected port restores automatic selection. Fixed Tailor Cable
fields and wide Focus Cable panels share this selection. Menus, help, Tailor
and active Port diagnostics retain keyboard priority. Cable arrows do not
also change process sorting; use Process sort in the settings menu instead.
Up/Down still selects processes. Switching Cable ports cancels pending TERM.
.PP
On Apple Silicon, the Cable view reads cable identity directly from the public
.B IOPortTransportComponentCCUSBPDSOPp
and SOP-double-prime IOKit services. It decodes the E-marker's cable type,
current and voltage rating, corresponding USB-PD power ceiling, data class,
numeric vendor/product IDs and PD revision. Static cable identity is refreshed
on connection or port changes and every five seconds while connected; live
charge and transport state keeps its faster polling interval. It also shows
the matching port's current data transports and whether DisplayPort Alt Mode
is active now, using
the directly reported AppleHPMInterface or fallback AppleTCController
TransportsActive list. For a direct USB
connection, the XHCI port's UsbIOPort path and exact locationID are matched to
the directly attached IOUSBHostDevice, whose reported UsbLinkSpeed supplies the
negotiated rate; ambiguous topology falls back to the broader USB2/USB3 name.
An inactive
DisplayPort state does not claim that the cable lacks DisplayPort capability,
and the Mac port's generic supported-transport list is not attributed to the
cable. Asmond does not infer cable capability from live charging current. If
macOS publishes no E-marker response, Asmond reports that no E-marker data is
available instead of guessing. Lingering E-marker data from a disconnected
port is not presented as the current cable. Tested Intel port controllers do
not expose this cable-identity path, so that view falls back to the available
Power Input or Battery view.
.PP
Memory bandwidth support is best-effort and depends on whether
.BR powermetrics (1)
exposes legacy
.B bandwidth_counters
fields on the local system.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred, for example missing
.BR powermetrics (1)
access or a failed probe command.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start the dashboard:
.B asmond
.TP
Start with a short sample interval:
.B asmond -i 0.2
.TP
Use the compact layout and Nord theme:
.B asmond --layout compact --theme nord
.TP
Show one parsed live sample:
.B asmond probe
.TP
Inspect raw exposed plist keys and values:
.B asmond probe --raw
.TP
Check local data sources:
.B asmond doctor
.TP
Create an anonymized support report:
.B asmond report
.TP
Create a report with one live powermetrics sample:
.B asmond report --live
.TP
Remove saved settings:
.B asmond --remove-settings
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR powermetrics (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR vm_stat (1),
.BR ioreg (8),
.BR sudo (8)
